# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title #
#, no-wrap
msgid "Small-scale simulator example {#small-scale-simulator-example}"
msgstr "Small-scale simulator example {#small-scale-simulator-example}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We use a small **max-cut** problem on a weighted graph as our running example. Max-cut asks: given a graph $G=(V,E)$ with edge weights $w_{ij}$, find a partition of the vertices into two sets $S$ and $\\bar{S}$ that maximizes the total weight of edges crossing the cut.\n"
msgstr "Gumagamit kami ng maliit na **max-cut** na problema sa isang weighted graph bilang aming running example. Itinatanong ng Max-cut: batay sa isang graph $G=(V,E)$ na may edge weights $w_{ij}$, hanapin ang isang partisyon ng mga vertex sa dalawang set na $S$ at $\\bar{S}$ na nagpapalaki sa kabuuang bigat ng mga edge na tumatawid sa cut.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As a QUBO minimization problem, max-cut can be written as:\n"
"$$\\min_{x \\in \\{0,1\\}^n} -\\sum_{(i,j) \\in E} w_{ij}(x_i + x_j - 2x_i x_j)$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Bilang isang QUBO minimization problem, ang max-cut ay maaaring isulat bilang:\n"
"$$\\min_{x \\in \\{0,1\\}^n} -\\sum_{(i,j) \\in E} w_{ij}(x_i + x_j - 2x_i x_j)$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We work with a four-node graph for tractability on a simulator.\n"
msgstr "Gumagamit kami ng four-node graph para sa tractability sa isang simulator.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"
msgstr "Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We define the max-cut problem using the `Maxcut` application class from `qiskit-addon-opt-mapper`, which builds the QUBO formulation directly from a graph. We then convert it to a QUBO and translate it to an Ising Hamiltonian (`SparsePauliOp`) suitable for QAOA. We also solve the continuous relaxation of the QUBO &mdash; replacing the binary constraint $x_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$ with $x_i \\in [0,1]$ &mdash; to obtain the warm-start initial point $c^*$.\n"
msgstr "Dine-define namin ang max-cut problem gamit ang `Maxcut` application class mula sa `qiskit-addon-opt-mapper`, na bumubuo ng QUBO formulation nang direkta mula sa graph. Pagkatapos ay kina-convert namin ito sa QUBO at isinasalin sa Ising Hamiltonian (`SparsePauliOp`) na angkop para sa QAOA. Nireresolba rin namin ang continuous relaxation ng QUBO &mdash; pinapalitan ang binary constraint na $x_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$ ng $x_i \\in [0,1]$ &mdash; para makuha ang warm-start initial point na $c^*$.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/step1-graph-code-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/step1-graph-code-0.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The graph has five edges. The optimal max-cut partitions the nodes into $S = \\{0, 3\\}$ and $\\bar{S} = \\{1, 2\\}$ (or its complement), severing four of the five edges for a cut value of 4.\n"
msgstr "Ang graph ay may limang edge. Ang optimal na max-cut ay naghihiwalay sa mga node patungo sa $S = \\{0, 3\\}$ at $\\bar{S} = \\{1, 2\\}$ (o ang complement nito), pinuputol ang apat sa limang edge para sa cut value na 4.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `Maxcut` class wraps the QUBO construction so we don't have to expand the max-cut objective by hand. The printed objective shows each variable's linear coefficient (how much it individually contributes to the cut) and each cross-term's quadratic coefficient (the penalty for putting two adjacent nodes on the same side). The underlying `OptimizationProblem` returned by `to_optimization_problem()` supports binary, integer, continuous, and spin variables, and is the same object expected by the converters and translators used in the next step.\n"
msgstr "Binabalot ng `Maxcut` class ang QUBO construction para hindi na namin kailangang i-expand ang max-cut objective nang manu-mano. Ipinapakita ng nakalimbag na objective ang linear coefficient ng bawat variable (kung gaano ito kontribuyente sa cut) at ang quadratic coefficient ng bawat cross-term (ang parusa sa paglalagay ng dalawang katabing node sa parehong panig). Ang pinagbabatayang `OptimizationProblem` na ibinabalik ng `to_optimization_problem()` ay sumusuporta sa binary, integer, continuous, at spin variables, at ito rin ang object na inaasahan ng mga converter at translator na ginagamit sa susunod na hakbang.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `to_ising` translator returns a `SparsePauliOp` representing $H_C$ and a scalar `offset` such that $\\text{QUBO value} = \\langle H_C \\rangle + \\text{offset}$. For this max-cut problem with all unit weights, $h_i = 0$ for all qubits (the graph is symmetric in linear terms after the $x_i \\to z_i$ substitution), and each edge contributes a $Z_i Z_j$ coupling of strength $+0.5$. The minimum eigenvalue of $H_C$ corresponds to the maximum cut.\n"
msgstr "Ang `to_ising` translator ay nagbabalik ng `SparsePauliOp` na kumakatawan sa $H_C$ at isang scalar na `offset` kung saan $\\text{QUBO value} = \\langle H_C \\rangle + \\text{offset}$. Para sa max-cut problem na ito na may lahat ng unit weight, $h_i = 0$ para sa lahat ng qubit (simetriko ang graph sa linear terms pagkatapos ng $x_i \\to z_i$ substitution), at ang bawat edge ay nag-aambag ng $Z_i Z_j$ coupling na may lakas na $+0.5$. Ang minimum eigenvalue ng $H_C$ ay tumutugma sa maximum cut.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The multi-start solver finds $c^* = [1, 0, 0, 1]$ (or its complement $[0, 1, 1, 0]$), which is the actual optimal binary solution. For this problem the QP relaxation is tight, the continuous minimum coincides with the integer optimum, meaning the relaxation immediately identifies the best cut. After regularization with $\\varepsilon = 0.25$ in Step 2, this solution will be encoded into the warm-start initial state.\n"
msgstr "Nahahanap ng multi-start solver ang $c^* = [1, 0, 0, 1]$ (o ang complement nitong $[0, 1, 1, 0]$), na siyang aktwal na optimal binary solution. Para sa problemang ito, mahigpit (tight) ang QP relaxation, ang continuous minimum ay nagtutugma sa integer optimum, ibig sabihin agad na natutukoy ng relaxation ang pinakamahusay na cut. Pagkatapos ng regularization gamit ang $\\varepsilon = 0.25$ sa Step 2, ang solusyong ito ay ie-encode sa warm-start initial state.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr "Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We build two QAOA circuits and prepare the warm-start angles from the QP solution.\n"
msgstr "Bumubuo kami ng dalawang QAOA circuits at inihahanda ang warm-start angles mula sa QP solution.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Standard QAOA** uses the uniform superposition $|+\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$ as the initial state and the standard $X$-mixer $H_M = -\\sum_i X_i$, implemented as $\\prod_i R_X(-2\\beta)$ per layer.\n"
msgstr "Ginagamit ng **Standard QAOA** ang uniform superposition $|+\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$ bilang initial state at ang standard $X$-mixer $H_M = -\\sum_i X_i$, na ipinatutupad bilang $\\prod_i R_X(-2\\beta)$ kada layer.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Warm-start QAOA (WS-QAOA)** from [\\[1\\]](#Reference1) makes two structural changes per qubit $i$:\n"
"- **Initial state:** $R_Y(\\theta_i)|0\\rangle$ with $\\theta_i = 2\\arcsin(\\sqrt{c^*_i})$, so the probability of measuring $|1\\rangle$ equals $c^*_i$.\n"
"- **Custom mixer:** $R_Y(\\theta_i)\\, R_Z(-2\\beta)\\, R_Y(-\\theta_i)$, which has $R_Y(\\theta_i)|0\\rangle$ as its ground state. This means WS-QAOA starts in the ground state of its own mixer, the same property that standard QAOA satisfies with $|+\\rangle$ and the $X$-mixer.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang **Warm-start QAOA (WS-QAOA)** mula sa [\\[1\\]](#Reference1) ay gumagawa ng dalawang structural change kada qubit $i$:\n"
"- **Initial state:** $R_Y(\\theta_i)|0\\rangle$ na may $\\theta_i = 2\\arcsin(\\sqrt{c^*_i})$, para ang probability ng pagsukat ng $|1\\rangle$ ay katumbas ng $c^*_i$.\n"
"- **Custom mixer:** $R_Y(\\theta_i)\\, R_Z(-2\\beta)\\, R_Y(-\\theta_i)$, na ang ground state ay $R_Y(\\theta_i)|0\\rangle$. Ibig sabihin nagsisimula ang WS-QAOA sa ground state ng sarili nitong mixer, ang parehong property na sinusunod ng standard QAOA sa $|+\\rangle$ at ang $X$-mixer.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note on layers: At `p=1`(a single QAOA layer), standard QAOA is analytically limited to ~49% of the optimal energy on graphs containing triangles (this graph has the triangle 0-1-2). The warm start bypasses this limitation by encoding prior knowledge of the solution directly into the initial state.\n"
msgstr "Tala tungkol sa layers: Sa `p=1` (iisang QAOA layer), limitado nang analytically ang standard QAOA sa ~49% ng optimal energy sa mga graph na may mga triangle (ang graph na ito ay may triangle na 0-1-2). Ang warm start ay lumalampas sa limitasyong ito sa pamamagitan ng pag-encode ng dating kaalaman tungkol sa solusyon nang direkta sa initial state.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "After clipping, $c^* = 1$ becomes $1 - \\varepsilon = 0.75$ and $c^* = 0$ becomes $\\varepsilon = 0.25$. The resulting angles $\\theta \\approx [2.09, 1.05, 1.05, 2.09]$ radians rotate qubits 0 and 3 strongly toward $|1\\rangle$ and qubits 1 and 2 toward $|0\\rangle$, directly encoding the structure of the optimal cut into the initial quantum state.\n"
msgstr "Pagkatapos ng clipping, ang $c^* = 1$ ay nagiging $1 - \\varepsilon = 0.75$ at ang $c^* = 0$ ay nagiging $\\varepsilon = 0.25$. Ang mga resultang angles na $\\theta \\approx [2.09, 1.05, 1.05, 2.09]$ radians ay umiikot sa mga qubit 0 at 3 nang husto patungo sa $|1\\rangle$ at sa mga qubit 1 at 2 patungo sa $|0\\rangle$, na direktang nage-encode ng estruktura ng optimal cut patungo sa initial quantum state.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For the standard ansatz we delegate to [`qaoa_ansatz`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.qaoa_ansatz), which constructs $|+\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, applies the cost unitary, and applies the default $X$-mixer for each of `reps` layers. For WS-QAOA we keep the explicit `build_ws_qaoa` helper because the per-qubit mixer $R_Y(\\theta)\\,R_Z(-2\\beta)\\,R_Y(-\\theta)$ is expressed as a gate sequence rather than as a sum of Paulis. The `apply_cost_unitary` helper reads directly from the `SparsePauliOp` Hamiltonian, so it handles any QUBO problem without manual circuit construction.\n"
msgstr "Para sa standard ansatz, ipinapasa namin ito sa [`qaoa_ansatz`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.qaoa_ansatz), na bumubuo ng $|+\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, nag-aaplay ng cost unitary, at nag-aaplay ng default na $X$-mixer para sa bawat isa sa `reps` na layer. Para sa WS-QAOA, pinananatili namin ang explicit na `build_ws_qaoa` helper dahil ang per-qubit mixer na $R_Y(\\theta)\\,R_Z(-2\\beta)\\,R_Y(-\\theta)$ ay inihahayag bilang isang gate sequence sa halip na isang sum ng Paulis. Ang `apply_cost_unitary` helper ay direktang nagbabasa mula sa `SparsePauliOp` Hamiltonian, kaya nahahandle nito ang kahit anong QUBO problem nang walang manu-manong circuit construction.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/step2-draw-code-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/step2-draw-code-1.avif)\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/6fad9eda-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/6fad9eda-1.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Both circuits follow the same structure: an initial state preparation layer, then $p$ alternating cost-unitary and mixer-unitary layers. In the WS-QAOA circuit, the opening $R_Y$ gates encode $c^*$, and the mixer replaces each $R_X$ with a conjugated $R_Y$–$R_Z$–$R_Y$ triplet. The circuit depth difference between the two grows linearly with $p$, but remains manageable at low depth.\n"
msgstr "Sumusunod ang parehong circuit sa parehong estruktura: isang initial state preparation layer, pagkatapos ay $p$ na nagsasalitang cost-unitary at mixer-unitary layers. Sa WS-QAOA circuit, ang unang mga $R_Y$ gates ay nage-encode ng $c^*$, at pinapalitan ng mixer ang bawat $R_X$ ng conjugated $R_Y$–$R_Z$–$R_Y$ triplet. Ang pagkakaiba sa circuit depth sa pagitan ng dalawa ay lumalaki nang linearly kasabay ng $p$, ngunit nananatiling madaling pamahalaan sa mababang depth.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-primitives}"
msgstr "Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-primitives}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We use `StatevectorEstimator` for exact, noiseless simulation. The `minimize` function from SciPy with the COBYLA optimizer drives the variational loop, calling the estimator at each iteration to evaluate $\\langle H_C \\rangle$ for a given parameter set $(\\gamma, \\beta)$.\n"
msgstr "Ginagamit namin ang `StatevectorEstimator` para sa eksakto, walang ingay (noiseless) na simulation. Ang `minimize` function mula sa SciPy na may COBYLA optimizer ang nagpapatakbo sa variational loop, na tumatawag sa estimator sa bawat iteration para sukatin ang $\\langle H_C \\rangle$ para sa isang naibigay na set ng parameter na $(\\gamma, \\beta)$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The two algorithms use different initial parameters that reflect what each knows before optimization:\n"
"- **Standard QAOA:** Random initialization in $[0, \\pi]$ — appropriate since no structural information is available.\n"
"- **WS-QAOA:** $\\gamma = 0$, $\\beta = \\pi/4$ — at $\\gamma=0$ the cost unitary is the identity, so the very first circuit evaluation samples directly from the warm-start initial state. This gives COBYLA a strong starting signal aligned with the classical solution.\n"
msgstr ""
"Gumagamit ang dalawang algorithm ng magkaibang initial parameters na sumasalamin sa kung ano ang alam ng bawat isa bago ang optimization:\n"
"- **Standard QAOA:** Random na initialization sa $[0, \\pi]$ — angkop dahil walang available na structural information.\n"
"- **WS-QAOA:** $\\gamma = 0$, $\\beta = \\pi/4$ — sa $\\gamma=0$, ang cost unitary ay ang identity, kaya ang mismong unang circuit evaluation ay direktang nagsa-sample mula sa warm-start initial state. Nagbibigay ito ng COBYLA ng malakas na starting signal na naaayon sa classical solution.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "WS-QAOA's informed starting point means COBYLA begins with a meaningful energy value near the warm-start solution, while standard QAOA starts from an essentially random point on the energy landscape. This difference in starting quality is the main driver of the convergence gap visible in Step 4.\n"
msgstr "Ang naimpormahang starting point ng WS-QAOA ay nangangahulugang nagsisimula ang COBYLA sa isang makabuluhang energy value malapit sa warm-start solution, samantalang ang standard QAOA ay nagsisimula sa isang halos random na punto sa energy landscape. Ang pagkakaibang ito sa kalidad ng starting point ang pangunahing dahilan ng convergence gap na makikita sa Step 4.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The approximation ratio is defined as $\\langle H_C \\rangle_{\\text{QAOA}} / E_{\\text{opt}}$. For minimization problems where $E_{\\text{opt}} < 0$, a ratio closer to 1 means the algorithm found a lower energy (a better solution). The brute-force search over all $2^n$ basis states is only feasible for small $n$ and serves as a ground-truth reference.\n"
msgstr "Ang approximation ratio ay dine-define bilang $\\langle H_C \\rangle_{\\text{QAOA}} / E_{\\text{opt}}$. Para sa mga minimization problem kung saan $E_{\\text{opt}} < 0$, ang ratio na mas malapit sa 1 ay nangangahulugang nakahanap ang algorithm ng mas mababang energy (mas mahusay na solusyon). Ang brute-force search sa lahat ng $2^n$ basis states ay feasible lang para sa maliit na $n$ at nagsisilbing ground-truth reference.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format {#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format}"
msgstr "Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format {#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We visualize convergence, sample the optimized circuits for bitstring solutions, decode those bitstrings back to max-cut partitions, and summarize the final results.\n"
msgstr "Vinivisualize namin ang convergence, sina-sample ang mga na-optimize na circuit para sa bitstring solutions, dinidecode ang mga bitstring na iyon pabalik sa max-cut partitions, at binubuod ang mga panghuling resulta.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/step4-convergence-code-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/step4-convergence-code-0.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The convergence plot shows the energy $\\langle H_C \\rangle$ at each COBYLA function evaluation. Standard QAOA at $p=1$ is limited to ~49% of the optimal energy on this graph (the theoretical maximum for $p=1$ QAOA on graphs with triangles), settling around $-0.74$. WS-QAOA, initialized close to the optimal solution, converges quickly to near $-1.50$ (the exact optimum) with far fewer iterations. This demonstrates the key advantage of the warm start: at the same circuit depth, it reaches a significantly better solution.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapakita ng convergence plot ang energy $\\langle H_C \\rangle$ sa bawat COBYLA function evaluation. Ang Standard QAOA sa $p=1$ ay limitado sa ~49% ng optimal energy sa graph na ito (ang theoretical maximum para sa $p=1$ QAOA sa mga graph na may triangle), na tumitigil sa paligid ng $-0.74$. Ang WS-QAOA, na na-initialize malapit sa optimal solution, ay mabilis na nagko-converge sa malapit sa $-1.50$ (ang eksaktong optimum) gamit ang mas kaunting iteration. Ipinapakita nito ang pangunahing bentahe ng warm start: sa parehong circuit depth, umaabot ito sa mas mahusay na solusyon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bitstrings from `Sampler` are returned with qubit 0 at the rightmost position, so reversing the string maps index $i$ to variable $x_i$. The cut value is the total weight of edges that cross the partition, which is what the max-cut problem aims to maximize. A cut value of 4 uses four of the five available edges, which is the theoretical maximum for this graph.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga bitstring mula sa `Sampler` ay ibinabalik na ang qubit 0 ay nasa pinakadulong kanan na posisyon, kaya ang pag-reverse sa string ay nagma-map ng index $i$ patungo sa variable $x_i$. Ang cut value ay ang kabuuang bigat ng mga edge na tumatawid sa partisyon, na siyang layunin na palakihin ng max-cut problem. Ang cut value na 4 ay gumagamit ng apat sa limang available na edge, na siyang theoretical maximum para sa graph na ito.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/step4-visualize-code-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/step4-visualize-code-0.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The graph visualization colors each node by its partition assignment (blue = $S$, orange = $\\bar{S}$). Edges that cross the partition (connecting differently-colored nodes) are the ones counted in the cut.\n"
msgstr "Ang graph visualization ay kinukulayan ang bawat node ayon sa partition assignment nito (blue = $S$, orange = $\\bar{S}$). Ang mga edge na tumatawid sa partisyon (kumokonekta sa mga node na magkaibang kulay) ang binibilang sa cut.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Both methods find a bitstring with cut value 4, but for very different reasons. It is important to note that the **convergence plot and the sampled bitstring measure two different things**:\n"
msgstr "Nakakahanap ang parehong metodo ng bitstring na may cut value na 4, ngunit dahil sa iba't ibang dahilan. Mahalagang tandaan na ang **convergence plot at ang sampled bitstring ay sumusukat ng dalawang magkaibang bagay**:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The **convergence plot** tracks the average energy $\\langle H_C \\rangle$ of the full quantum state, a weighted average over all bitstrings in the superposition. Standard QAOA converges to ~$-0.62$, well above the optimal $-1.50$, meaning its quantum state is spread across many suboptimal bitstrings and only occasionally includes the correct answer.\n"
msgstr "Sinusubaybayan ng **convergence plot** ang average energy $\\langle H_C \\rangle$ ng buong quantum state, isang weighted average sa lahat ng bitstring sa superposition. Ang Standard QAOA ay nagko-converge sa halos $-0.62$, malayong-malayo sa optimal na $-1.50$, ibig sabihin ang quantum state nito ay kumalat sa maraming suboptimal na bitstring at bihira lang isinasama ang tamang sagot.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The **sampled bitstring** is a single draw from that state. Standard QAOA got lucky here; the optimal partition happened to be the most frequently sampled outcome even from a diffuse state. On harder problems, noisier hardware, or with more candidate solutions competing, this luck runs out.\n"
msgstr "Ang **sampled bitstring** ay isang solong draw mula sa state na iyon. Suwerte ang Standard QAOA dito; ang optimal partition ay nagkataong siyang pinakamadalas na sampled outcome kahit mula sa isang diffuse na state. Sa mas mahihirap na problema, mas maingay na hardware, o may mas maraming kandidatong solusyon na nakikipagkumpetisyon, mauubos ang suwerteng ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "WS-QAOA, by contrast, converges its average energy all the way to $-1.50$, meaning its quantum state is concentrated on the optimal bitstrings. Nearly every shot returns the correct answer, so the solution is found reliably rather than by chance.\n"
msgstr "Sa kabaligtaran, ang WS-QAOA ay nagko-converge ang average energy nito hanggang sa $-1.50$, ibig sabihin ang quantum state nito ay concentrated sa mga optimal na bitstring. Halos bawat shot ay nagbabalik ng tamang sagot, kaya ang solusyon ay matatagpuan nang maaasahan sa halip na dahil sa suwerte.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The practical consequence: on this small noiseless simulator the difference may seem minor, but at larger problem sizes or on real hardware, a state with average energy close to optimal is far more robust than one that only occasionally samples the right answer from a diffuse distribution.\n"
msgstr "Ang praktikal na kahihinatnan: sa maliit na walang ingay na simulator na ito, maaaring maliit lang ang pagkakaiba, ngunit sa mas malalaking problema o sa aktwal na hardware, mas matatag ang isang state na may average energy na malapit sa optimal kaysa sa isang state na paminsan-minsan lang nagsa-sample ng tamang sagot mula sa diffuse na distribution.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/b01696c2-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/warm-start-qaoa/extracted-outputs/b01696c2-0.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This histogram quantifies what the convergence plot only suggested. Standard QAOA's probability is spread across multiple suboptimal cut values, so the chance of sampling an optimal cut of four in any single shot is only a fraction of the total mass. WS-QAOA concentrates nearly all of its probability on the optimal cut, so almost every shot returns the right answer. This is the practical signature of a state whose average energy has converged to the ground-state energy versus one that has merely happened to include the ground state in a broad superposition.\n"
msgstr "Kinukwantipika ng histogram na ito ang minumungkahi lang ng convergence plot. Ang probability ng Standard QAOA ay kumakalat sa maraming suboptimal na cut values, kaya ang tsansa ng pag-sample ng optimal na cut na apat sa kahit anong iisang shot ay isang bahagi lang ng kabuuang mass. Ang WS-QAOA ay ikinokonsentra ang halos lahat ng probability nito sa optimal na cut, kaya halos bawat shot ay nagbabalik ng tamang sagot. Ito ang praktikal na palatandaan ng isang state na ang average energy ay nagko-converge patungo sa ground-state energy laban sa isa na nagkataon lang na naisama ang ground state sa isang malawak na superposition.\n"

